{"title":"Argument","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Given a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_number\" title=\"Complex number\">complex number</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, the <b>argument</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/3/ff384734af3c135f660303a3b612c4513cf4b435.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"11\" /> is the measure of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Signed_angle\" title=\"Signed angle\">signed angle</a> the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ray\" title=\"Ray\">ray</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/a/72a3b2663958da75acf45b10c4c4dab7ed179e72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{0z}$\" width=\"17\" height=\"21\" /> makes with the positive <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Real_axis\" title=\"Real axis\">real axis</a>.  (Note that this means the argument of the complex number 0 is undefined.)\n</p><p>Unfortunately, this means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7eb82f7618f4e9ffd538a08d807749d1a74ae28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"11\" /> is not a proper <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Function\" title=\"Function\">function</a> but is instead a \"multi-valued function\": for example, any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Positive\">positive</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Real_number\" title=\"Real number\">real number</a> has argument 0, but also has argument <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11d640b2957eee0e62b810fd314a5493e7b2c027.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pi, -2\\pi, 4\\pi, \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />.  This means that the argument may be best considered as an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Equivalence_class\" title=\"Equivalence class\">equivalence class</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/e/4dee14635fb785fc8caa4a340ddcd1bcdea8d344.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf r = \\{r + 2\\pi n, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"165\" height=\"18\" />.  The advantages of this are several: most importantly, they make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7eb82f7618f4e9ffd538a08d807749d1a74ae28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"11\" /> into a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continuous_function\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Continuous function\">continuous function</a>.  They also make some properties of the argument \"look nicer.\"  For example, under this interpretation, we can write <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/21495467959e422ee3636e469dfe9f107a4a6560.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg(w \\cdot z) = \\arg(w) + \\arg(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"222\" height=\"18\" />.  The other common solution is to restrict the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Range\" title=\"Range\">range</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7eb82f7618f4e9ffd538a08d807749d1a74ae28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"11\" /> to some <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interval\" title=\"Interval\">interval</a>, usually <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/5212cf082a56e5bfd4ec65534477c18d650b46a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0, 2\\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/2/e520a7aeab7e3c5078aebf7d07340c9485d2a110.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-\\pi, \\pi]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />.  This forces us to state this equality <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Modulo\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Modulo\">modulo</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/9/709acef3551b886e6754de72d59a88f00660f0d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>The argument of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is undefined.\n</p><p>When a complex number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is expressed in polar form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/7/f07080129914ac008f0eb45ed5f7efa28bb1e7c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(r,\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the argument of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Finding_Argument\">Finding Argument</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Argument&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Finding Argument\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Take the complex number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46b245331cc3cccf7ccaf7e2f6a34fa2131b8b33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=a+bi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" />. Then we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5bad7fc1efff1b8da42755d9bd78bc9e8cd12a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arctan\\frac{b}{a}=\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"39\" />. Since arctan does not cover all angles, we have four cases:\n</p><p>1. The complex number is in the first quadrant (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/c/dac979b9a676f754201f4d8003513f6177d04586.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&gt;0, b&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />). <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/9/b5998ab427cc57b46d9a09ba04e49aa3f6fafebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg(z)=\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"84\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>2. The complex number is in the first quadrant (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/d/5cddc8245b944934b86ad5b5daa4e9b9a7063909.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;0, b&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />). <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/3/453dce5debf4a05f5a17a17413bd678fb1f5e4b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg(z)=\\pi-\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>3. The complex number is in the first quadrant (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/8/178ae6a689c932f4a7168d7f63ebacad2215d0d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;0, b&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />). <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/9/d29fd575b0a2e02f072ce8c08b2ef1827e036b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg(z)=\\pi+\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>4. The complex number is in the first quadrant (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/1/e9153c7a37834972182359b90e9aed287748940c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&gt;0, b&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />). <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/93476f915f4e91142bfb7e01114ab2f41a89ebca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg(z)=2\\pi-\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>And of course, when the complex number is on one of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Axes\" title=\"Axes\">axes</a>, then the argument will just be the angle of the axis.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Argument&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018073432\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.020 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 136/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 265/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4912/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.320      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.320      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3938:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018073432 and revision id 281797. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}